# CI / CD — opt-in by provider

Lazurite does not activate CI/CD by default — silent activation on first push (consuming minutes, sending failure notifications, blocking PRs you didn't expect) violates the principle of least surprise. Pick your provider below and run the one-line activation command.

All three provider configs run **the same strict gate** as the local `pnpm verify:all` chain:

1. `lazuli check` — schema fast-fail (~5s)
2. `lazuli doctor --security-profile strict --coverage` — strict + per-layer coverage (~20s)
3. `lazuli generate` + `go build` — emitted Go compiles (~30s)
4. `pnpm typecheck` — emitted TS typechecks (~20s)
5. `db migrate` — schema matches production
6. `lazuli test --layer handler` — handler tests vs real Postgres (variable)

Green locally ⇒ green on CI.

## GitHub Actions

```bash
mkdir -p .github && mv scripts/ci/github .github/workflows
git add .github/workflows && git commit -m "ci: activate strict gate"
```

Activates two workflows:
- **`lazuli.yml`** — runs on every push and PR
- **`release.yml`** — runs on `v*.*.*` tags (deploy step is a stub — wire it to your target)

## GitLab CI

```bash
mv scripts/ci/gitlab/.gitlab-ci.yml ./.gitlab-ci.yml
git add .gitlab-ci.yml && git commit -m "ci: activate strict gate"
```

Single pipeline runs on every push and MR. Uses GitLab's built-in cache for pnpm/cargo/go.

## Bitbucket Pipelines

```bash
mv scripts/ci/bitbucket/bitbucket-pipelines.yml ./bitbucket-pipelines.yml
git add bitbucket-pipelines.yml && git commit -m "ci: activate strict gate"
```

Single pipeline runs on every push and PR. Heads-up: Bitbucket bills per build minute — see the file header for free-tier scoping hints.

## Other CI providers (Jenkins, CircleCI, Drone, Buildkite, …)

The gate boils down to four steps. Wire them in your provider's syntax:

```sh
# 1. Install Lazuli CLI (until a release binary ships, install from source)
cargo install --git https://github.com/lazuli-lang/lazuli --locked lazuli_cli

# 2. Install JS deps
pnpm install --frozen-lockfile

# 3. Run migrations against a real Postgres
LAZULI_TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://USER:PASS@HOST:5432/DB?sslmode=disable \
  pnpm db:migrate

# 4. Run the full strict gate
LAZULI_TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://USER:PASS@HOST:5432/DB?sslmode=disable \
  pnpm verify:all
```

Cache: `~/.cargo/bin/lazuli`, `~/.pnpm-store`, `~/.cache/go-build`, and your Cargo registry — these dominate cold-start time.

Provide a Postgres 16 instance via service container, sidecar, or external — the URL the gate reads is `LAZULI_TEST_DATABASE_URL`.

## How to soften a gate

**Never edit the CI files.** Author a `[doctor.test_discipline.severity_override."<RULE-CODE>"]` entry in `Lazurite.toml` with a mandatory `reason` (enforced by `DOCTOR-OVERRIDE-NEEDS-REASON-001`). That way the override is reviewable per PR/MR — anyone reading the diff sees both the override and the reason at the same time.

Example:

```toml
[doctor.test_discipline.severity_override."TEST-STUB-001"]
severity = "warning"
reason   = "tracked refactor 2026-06; restoring strict after #142 lands"
```
